PUBLIC RESPONSE: C

The blanks are consistent in every extant transcription, so any viable explanation has to operate on the archetype that all later copies descend from, not on each copyist acting independently.

A, B, D, E, F and G can all be made to fit that constraint in principle.

* A is a motive-based authorial redaction before presentation;
* B is a norm-based scribal omission that would be reproduced by later copyists copying an already blank archetype;
* D is institutional redaction by the Oniwaban of the archetype;
* E is the archetype really containing aburidashi invisible-ink writing, which a non-ninja copyist would see as blank and therefore reproduce as blank circles;
* F is the archetype containing a mnemonic colour code that a copyist lacking the oral transmission would reduce to circles;
* G is physical loss of the archetype in the heavily consulted Kunoichi section, with later copyists faithfully marking the lacuna with placeholders.

C requires a historically unsubstantiated personal conspiracy to be both possible and effective. There is no credible Tokugawa-era record of a “Lady Saigō” who was a key shogunal figure and the stepdaughter of Hattori, let alone of her using kunoichi methods to sway shogunal politics. The causal chain needed — that her private tactics threatened the legitimacy of the Tokugawa line and the Pax Tokugawa, and that she or her agents could locate and destroy evidence of them in the master text of the Bansenshukai and prevent that redacted master from being the source for all later transcriptions — is ad hoc, anachronistic and unsupported by the documentary history of Fujibayashi Masataka’s 1676 compilation and of the Oniwaban archival practice. It is the only option that invents a specific, historically implausible actor and motive while failing to explain how a private individual could impose a uniform lacuna on every surviving lineage of copies.

The other options, however speculative, at least map onto known Edo-period practices: shogunal secrecy and Oniwaban censorship, social modesty censorship of women’s manuals, the real aburidashi heat-reveal technique, oral transmission with mnemonic diagrams, and archetype degradation being faithfully reproduced.

